# Target C-II — diameter thrackle paths have a common extended complement cell

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only exact finite structural theorem  
**Scope:** any pairwise-noncrossing O--O train family to which the audited proper-crosscut complement-dual tree construction applies  
**Dependencies:** audited O--O proper-crosscut/complement-dual tree topology; elementary collar extension of a disk; no KKT coefficient bound  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The live O--O backbone theorem reduces every actual diameter row to one signed interval
of endpoint-shore incidence on a dual-tree path.  There is an additional exact
geometric constraint: after extending the audited train disk by a collar to one large
convex disk containing the full source, all actual straight diameter segments meet one
common **extended complement cell** of the noncrossing train family.

The reason is a composition of three finite facts.

1. Straight diameter segments in the plane form a geometric thrackle: any two share an
   endpoint or cross.
2. Pairwise-disjoint proper crosscuts of the audited train disk extend through a collar
   to pairwise-disjoint proper crosscuts of a large convex disk, without changing their
   complement-dual tree.
3. For one connected curve in that large disk, the set of complement cells whose
   closures meet the curve is a connected subtree of the dual.

Pairwise-intersecting subtrees of a tree have the Helly property.  Therefore all
actual diameter trace subtrees contain one common dual vertex.

The collar repair is load-bearing.  The audited train disk need not be convex, so an
actual straight diameter chord can leave it.  The first draft of this theorem silently
assumed otherwise.  The extended-disk formulation removes that gap.

The theorem does **not** assert that the endpoint-shore interval of every diameter row
contains the common vertex.  A straight diameter segment can in principle meet one
proper train crosscut more than once, so its geometric trace subtree can be larger than
the unique dual path between its endpoint regions.

---

## 1. Any two planar diameter segments intersect

Let `X` be a finite planar set of diameter `D`, and let `ab` and `cd` be two distinct
unordered diameter pairs:

\[
|a-b|=|c-d|=D.
\]

If they share an endpoint there is nothing to prove.  Suppose their four endpoints are
distinct.

Every endpoint of a diameter pair is an exposed vertex of `conv(X)`: for example, if
`b-a=r` with `|r|=D`, then for every `x in X`,

\[
|x-b|^2\le D^2=|a-b|^2
\]

gives

\[
(x-a)\cdot r\ge {|x-a|^2\over2}.
\]

Thus `-r` is a strict outward supporting normal at `a`, and similarly for the other
three endpoints.  Hence `a,b,c,d` are in strict convex position.

Assume for contradiction that the two segments `ab` and `cd` do not meet.  In the
cyclic order of their four endpoints they are then opposite sides of the convex
quadrilateral.  Let `o` be the intersection of its two diagonals.  Strict triangle
inequality gives

\[
|a-b|<|a-o|+|o-b|,
\]

\[
|c-d|<|c-o|+|o-d|.
\]

Adding,

\[
2D
<(|a-o|+|o-c|)+(|b-o|+|o-d|)
=|a-c|+|b-d|.
\]

At least one diagonal therefore has length greater than `D`, contradiction.

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\text{any two actual diameter segments share an endpoint or cross.}
}
\tag{DH.1}

No external diameter-graph theorem is used.

---

## 2. Extend the train disk to a convex ambient disk

Let `B` be the audited closed topological train disk for a pairwise-disjoint proper
O--O family

\[
\mathcal L=\{\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m\}.
\]

Write `gamma_i` for the proper train centerline crosscut representing `tau_i`.
The complement-region/crosscut dual in `B` is the audited tree `T_L`.

Choose a sufficiently large closed Euclidean round disk `K` whose interior contains
`B` and the entire finite source.  Then

\[
K\setminus\operatorname{int}B
\]

is a topological annulus.  Give it a collar coordinate

\[
\partial B\times[0,1],
\]

with `partial B x {0}` identified with `partial B` and `partial B x {1}` with
`partial K`.  Such a collar follows directly from Jordan--Schoenflies for the two disk
boundaries.

Every proper crosscut `gamma_i` has two distinct endpoints on `partial B`.  Extend each
endpoint along its vertical collar fiber to `partial K`.  Distinct endpoint fibers are
disjoint, so the resulting arcs

\[
\widetilde\gamma_i
\]

are pairwise-disjoint proper crosscuts of `K`.  Inside `B` they coincide with the
original train crosscuts.

Adding the collar does not change the complement-dual combinatorics.  Each original
complement region acquires exactly the collar sectors adjacent to its boundary
intervals; no two original regions merge across a vertical extension fiber.  Thus the
complement dual of

\[
K\setminus\bigcup_i\widetilde\gamma_i
\]

is canonically the same tree `T_L`.

We call these enlarged complement components the **extended cells**.  Every source
point lies in `K`, and every actual straight diameter segment lies in `K` because `K`
is convex.

---

## 3. Region trace of one connected curve is a subtree

For any connected compact curve `s subset K`, define

\[
\mathcal R(s)
:=\{R\in V(T_L):\overline{\widetilde R}\cap s\ne\varnothing\},
\tag{DH.2}
\]

where `widetilde R` is the extended cell labelled by dual vertex `R`.

### Lemma DH.1 — `R(s)` is connected in `T_L`

Take `R_1,R_2 in R(s)` and let

\[
R_1=Q_0,Q_1,\ldots,Q_k=R_2
\]

be their unique dual-tree path.  Fix one edge `Q_{j-1}Q_j`, represented by the proper
crosscut `widetilde gamma_j`.  Removing that crosscut separates `K` into the two unions
of extended cells represented by the two components of
`T_L-Q_{j-1}Q_j`.  The cells `R_1,R_2` lie on opposite sides.

Choose points of `s` in the two cell closures.  Since `s` is connected, it meets the
separating crosscut.  At a point of intersection, both incident cell closures meet
`s`, so both `Q_{j-1}` and `Q_j` belong to `R(s)`.

Doing this for every path edge proves that all `Q_j` belong to `R(s)`.  Hence

\[
\boxed{\mathcal R(s)\text{ is a subtree of }T_L.}
\tag{DH.3}

Tangencies and overlaps cause no problem because cell closures are used.

---

## 4. Diameter trace subtrees are pairwise intersecting

For an actual diameter pair `h`, let `s_h` be its straight Euclidean segment and put

\[
T_h:=\mathcal R(s_h).
\]

Every `s_h` lies in the convex ambient disk `K`, so Lemma DH.1 applies.

Take two actual diameter pairs `h,g`.  By (DH.1), their straight segments meet at some
point `p`.  Choose any extended cell `widetilde R` whose closure contains `p`.  Then

\[
p\in\overline{\widetilde R}\cap s_h
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
p\in\overline{\widetilde R}\cap s_g,
\]

so

\[
R\in T_h\cap T_g.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{T_h\cap T_g\ne\varnothing\quad\text{for all diameter pairs }h,g.}
\tag{DH.4}

A shared diameter endpoint is harmless: at least one extended cell closure contains
that boundary or interior source point.

---

## 5. Tree Helly gives one common diameter cell

Finite connected subgraphs of a tree satisfy the Helly property: pairwise intersection
implies total intersection.

For completeness, root `T_L` arbitrarily.  For a subtree `S`, let `v(S)` be its unique
vertex nearest the root.  Choose `S_*` with `v(S_*)` farthest from the root.  For every
other subtree `S`, choose `x in S cap S_*`.  Both nearest-root vertices lie on the
root-to-`x` path, and maximal depth puts `v(S_*)` between `v(S)` and `x`.  Since `S`
is connected, it contains that entire tree segment and therefore contains `v(S_*)`.
Thus `v(S_*)` belongs to every subtree.

Apply this to `{T_h}`.  There is a dual vertex `R_*` such that

\[
R_*\in T_h
\qquad\text{for every actual diameter pair }h.
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
\overline{\widetilde R_*}\cap s_h\ne\varnothing
\quad\text{for every actual diameter segment }s_h.
}
\tag{DH.5}

So the extended crosscut arrangement contains one cell closure pierced by all actual
straight diameter segments.

This is a cell-piercing theorem, not a common-point theorem: the diameter segments need
not all meet at one Euclidean point.

---

## 6. Projection to a live backbone

Let

\[
P=(Q_0,e_1,Q_1,\ldots,e_m,Q_m)
\]

be any simple path in `T_L`, for example the current `0.017104D-o(D)` live smallest-
leaf O--O backbone.  Let `Q_*` be the unique nearest vertex of `P` to `R_*`.

If one diameter segment `s_h` meets any selected extended backbone crosscut, then
`T_h` meets `P`.  Since `T_h` is connected and contains `R_*`, the unique tree path
from `R_*` to `T_h cap P` passes through `Q_*`; hence

\[
\boxed{Q_*\in T_h.}
\tag{DH.6}

If the two endpoints of `h` lie on opposite shores of one selected train cut, they also
lie on opposite sides of its extended proper crosscut in `K`.  The straight segment is
connected and therefore meets that crosscut.  Thus every diameter row with nonzero
shore-incidence support on the backbone has an extended-cell trace containing `Q_*`.

This is additional information beyond raw interval support: every active row has a
straight geometric representative passing through the closure of one common **extended
backbone cell label**.

It still does not follow that the endpoint-region interval of that row contains
`Q_*`; multiple crossings of one wavy proper crosscut are not excluded.

---

## 7. Interaction with the live interval-rank theorem

The current live `t<=1.844` theorem supplies a backbone of more than

\[
0.017104D-o(D)
\]

smallest-leaf O--O cuts.  It proves:

- the diameter derivative matrix on those cuts has full column rank;
- at least `m-2` diameter rows have proper nonempty endpoint-shore intervals;
- incidence switches occur at linearly many distinct internal strip boundaries; and
- the KKT resultant sequence has total variation at most `2D`.

The present theorem adds a different exact constraint: all active straight diameter
chords meet the closure of one common extended-cell label `Q_*` after the canonical
collar extension of the same train cuts.

Thus the brace system is simultaneously

```text
interval-supported in endpoint-shore coordinates
+
full-rank on >0.017104 D selected cuts
+
straight-chord thrackle geometry
+
one common extended dual cell pierced by every active chord.
```

The next metric obligation is to convert the common-cell piercing into a restriction
on the directions or endpoint projection positions of the switching chords.  The
current theorem itself does not provide such a bound.

---

## 8. Scope audit and explicit non-implications

- The train disk need not be convex.  The collar extension to a large convex disk is
  therefore part of the theorem, not a cosmetic reformulation.
- The complement dual is used only for a pairwise-noncrossing proper O--O family, where
  the tree model is already audited.
- The collar extension preserves the dual tree but enlarges its geometric cells.  Thus
  `DH.5` concerns an **extended cell**, not necessarily the original rhombic/contact
  region inside the train disk.
- Region closures are essential for tangencies and shared endpoints.
- The theorem proves one common extended cell, not one common Euclidean point.
- It does not assert that a diameter chord crosses a train crosscut at most once.
- Consequently it does not assert that endpoint-shore support intervals are rooted at
  `Q_*`.
- No KKT multiplier lower bound, response inequality, or phase-collapse estimate is
  used.
- The theorem is exact and finite.  Its application to the `0.017104D` backbone inherits
  that backbone theorem's proof-only `t<=1.844` scope.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only exact diameter-thrackle / dual-Helly common extended
cell theorem. C-II and Level I remain open.
